module

public import StacksFrontier.Algebra.Category.ModuleCat.Sheaf.OnSpaceMonoidalPreadditive
public import StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.DerivedVarieties.ExternalTensor.DualHom

/-!
# The duality bijections of hom-groups are additive, and linear over an affine base

`AlgebraicGeometry.dualTensorHomEquiv` is the hom-set form of the left-duality of a finite
locally free `𝒪_W`-module `P`,

`Hom_{𝒪_W}(P, ℱ) ≃ Γ(W, ℱ ⊗ P^∨)`,

and `AlgebraicGeometry.externalTensorDualTensorHomEquiv` is the same bijection at
`P = ℰ ⊠ 𝒢`, read through `(ℰ ⊠ 𝒢)^∨ ≅ ℰ^∨ ⊠ 𝒢^∨`. Both are `Equiv`s and not `AddEquiv`s,
because `CategoryTheory.tensorRightHomEquiv`, which they are built from, records no additivity in
Mathlib. This file proves that both are additive.

That is what a *spanning* statement needs. A bijection of hom-groups transports surjectivity of
any map onto one of them for free; what it does not transport, unless it is additive, is
surjectivity of an *additive* map — the image of a subgroup is a subgroup only across an additive
bijection, and a statement "every morphism out of `ℰ ⊠ 𝒢` is a sum of morphisms of a given
family" is exactly a statement about the subgroup generated by that family. The implementation
notes of
`StacksFrontier/AlgebraicGeometry/DerivedVarieties/TransformedDiagonal/Kunneth.lean` record the
absence of this additivity as one of the obstructions between the Künneth formula for Ext of
[Stacks, Tag 0FXZ] and the spanning statements the complex of [Stacks, Tag 0G0A] is carried on.

When the base is affine, `Spec R`, the hom-groups are `R`-modules by
`AlgebraicGeometry.modulesLinear` and both bijections are `R`-linear and not merely additive.
That is proved here too, and it is what a spanning statement about the *bilinearity* relations
needs: those relations are the `R`-linear ones, and an additive bijection carries only the
subgroup generated by sums.

## Main results

* `CategoryTheory.tensorRightHomEquiv_add`: in a monoidal preadditive category the hom-set
  bijection of an exact pairing is additive.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.modulesMonoidalPreadditive`: the tensor product of modules on a scheme
  is biadditive, at the spelling `AlgebraicGeometry.onSpaceModulesMonoidal` the duality
  statements use.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.dualTensorHomEquiv_apply`: the duality bijection written out, as the
  hom-set bijection of the pairing between `P` and `P^∨` precomposed with the left unitor.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.dualTensorHomEquiv_add` and
  `AlgebraicGeometry.externalTensorDualTensorHomEquiv_add`: **the two duality bijections are
  additive.**
* `CategoryTheory.tensorRightHomEquiv_smul`: the same bijection is `R`-linear when the tensor
  product is `R`-bilinear on morphisms.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.smul_id_whiskerRight`, `whiskerLeft_smul_id` and
  `AlgebraicGeometry.modulesMonoidalLinear`: the tensor product of modules on an `R`-scheme is
  `R`-bilinear on morphisms, which is `CategoryTheory.MonoidalLinear` for
  `AlgebraicGeometry.onSpaceModulesMonoidal`.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.dualTensorHomEquiv_smul` and
  `AlgebraicGeometry.externalTensorDualTensorHomEquiv_smul`: **the two duality bijections are
  `R`-linear** over an affine base.

## Implementation notes

`CategoryTheory.tensorRightHomEquiv X Y Y' Z` sends `f` to
`(ρ_ X).inv ≫ X ◁ η_ Y Y' ≫ (α_ X Y Y').inv ≫ f ▷ Y'`, so its additivity is
`CategoryTheory.MonoidalPreadditive.add_whiskerRight` followed by additivity of composition. The
biadditivity it asks for is
`Stacks.SheafOfModules.OnSpace.monoidalPreadditive`, re-ascribed here at
`AlgebraicGeometry.Scheme.Modules`: instance search does not unfold that `def`, which is the
same reason `AlgebraicGeometry.onSpaceModulesMonoidal` has to be named separately from
`Stacks.SheafOfModules.OnSpace.monoidal`.

Neither `AlgebraicGeometry.onSpaceModulesMonoidal` nor the biadditivity is a global instance — the
modules of a scheme carry several monoidal structures with the same isomorphism class and
different terms — so both are activated with `attribute [local instance]`, as in
`StacksFrontier/AlgebraicGeometry/DerivedVarieties/ExternalTensor/DualHom.lean`.

The exact pairing between `P` and `P^∨` is
`AlgebraicGeometry.VectorBundleKZero.dualSheafExactPairing`, which takes the finite local freeness
as an argument and is therefore not an instance; it is passed explicitly, as it is in the
definition of `AlgebraicGeometry.dualTensorHomEquiv` itself.
That is why `AlgebraicGeometry.dualTensorHomEquiv_add` finishes by applying
`CategoryTheory.tensorRightHomEquiv_add` with all of its instances given rather than by
rewriting with it: a rewrite has to match the pairing in the goal, which carries the
`Stacks.SheafOfModules` spelling of the category while the statement carries the
`AlgebraicGeometry.Scheme.Modules` one. `AlgebraicGeometry.dualTensorHomEquiv_smul` does the
same, with `AlgebraicGeometry.modulesLinear` and `AlgebraicGeometry.modulesMonoidalLinear`
supplied in the same way.

`AlgebraicGeometry.modulesMonoidalLinear` is proved from its two values at `c • 𝟙` rather than
by a computation on a general morphism, because a scalar *is* `c • 𝟙` composed: the linear
structure of `AlgebraicGeometry.modulesLinear` is `CategoryTheory.Linear.ofRingMorphism` at
multiplication by the image of the scalar among the global functions. Those two values are
`AlgebraicGeometry.tensorSheafMap_globalSmulCenter_left` and `_right` of
`StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.ResolutionsAndBounds.ExternalTensorFunctorial`, which know
nothing of a base ring.

`AlgebraicGeometry.externalTensorDualTensorHomEquiv_smul` states the fibre product's linear
structure as `AlgebraicGeometry.modulesLinear` at `AlgebraicGeometry.externalTensorOver`, the
fibre product read as an `R`-scheme through its first projection, and introduces the value of
`AlgebraicGeometry.dualTensorHomEquiv_smul` there as a `have` with the type written out at the
`CategoryTheory.Limits.pullback` spelling. Without that the rewrite does not fire: the statement
of `dualTensorHomEquiv_smul` carries `(externalTensorOver R X Y).left`, and `rw` does not unfold
the `CategoryTheory.Over.mk` that stands between the two.

